WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:09.710
Hello! This is PampaVision, a master's project in the LIAA course at PPGES, Unipampa, in Brazil.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:17.430
In the next three minutes, I'll show you the problem, the proposal, the data, the results, and what comes next.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:23.120
Every vehicle inspection produces photos of the front and the back of the car.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:32.950
Someone has to check whether the model and the color in the photo match the registry. Done by hand, that is slow, tiring, and error-prone.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.470
The proposal is to use computer vision for this check.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:50.490
The network recognizes the model and the color in the photo and compares them with the registry. When they match, the inspection moves on. When they don't, the photo goes to a human reviewer.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:56.660
We started with 188,000 photos, in 38 packages.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.220
A vision-language model, running on an internal server, selected the photos with the hood closed.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:12.470
After curation, we kept 74,149 photos of 38 models, 9 makes, and 13 colors.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:16.070
And curating data teaches a lot.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:27.310
We found photos of photos, photos without the car, and registry colors that don't match reality: an entire model registered as beige shows up white in almost every photo.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:31.980
So we paired the photos by vehicle and recorded every decision.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:40.360
For recognition, we trained two YOLO classification networks: one for the model, the other for the color.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:46.920
Training ran on an H100 GPU: 500 epochs in just over three hours.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:57.600
On the test set, with more than 7,000 unseen photos, accuracy was 95.1% for the model and 93.6% for the color.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.270
And the make is right in 99.4% of cases.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:09.210
Notice where the network fails: almost 9 out of 10 model errors stay within the same make.

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:17.330
The biggest confusion is between hatchback and sedan, like the Onix and the Prisma, which look almost identical from the front.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:24.800
We also compared three annotation tools: CVAT, Label Studio, and Roboflow.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.490
And we created one-page guides for the people who take the photos.

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:37.440
Looking ahead to the field, we built a mobile app prototype and this website, which follows the project's progress.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:44.560
Throughout, we follow Brazil's data protection law: license plates and chassis numbers never appear.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:52.460
The next steps are to validate in the field, review conflicting labels, and better separate hatchback from sedan.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.190
Thank you for watching, and see you next time!
